<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean Froissart</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1337–c. 1405</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean Froissart was a poet and chronicler from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valenciennes">Valenciennes</a> in Hainaut. He entered the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippa-of-hainaut">Philippa of Hainaut</a>, queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in the 1360s and later found patrons in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. His Chronicles, describing the first half of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a> and the chivalric world of England, France and the Low Countries, are the best-known narrative of the age.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-froissart.jpg" alt="Jean Froissart" width="320" height="485" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jacques Le Boucq · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean-Froissart.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Froissart's life, and it comes mainly from his historical writings and from archives that mention him in the service of aristocrats or receiving gifts from them. His poems were once used to reconstruct his biography, but this approach is now said to be flawed, since the I of many poems should not be read as a reliable reference to the author. Froissart came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valenciennes">Valenciennes</a> in the County of Hainaut, at the western tip of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>. Earlier scholars suggested that his father painted armorial bearings and that he began as a merchant before becoming a cleric, but the evidence for both is questionable.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At about 24 Froissart left Hainaut and entered the service of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippa-of-hainaut">Philippa of Hainaut</a>, queen consort of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a>, in 1361 or 1362. This service is often presented as an official post of court poet or historiographer. Croenen, from the surviving English court archives, concluded that it was no official position and was probably more a literary construction, in which a poet dedicated poems to his lady and received occasional gifts. He travelled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wales">Wales</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/flanders">Flanders</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> gathering material and first-hand accounts for his Chronicles. He went with Lionel, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glarentza">Clarence</a>, to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> to attend and chronicle the duke's wedding to Violante, daughter of Galeazzo Visconti, where Chaucer and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> were also present.</p><p>After Queen Philippa's death in 1369 he enjoyed the patronage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joanna-duchess-of-brabant">Joanna, Duchess of Brabant</a>, among others. His rewards included the benefice of Estinnes, near Binche, and later a canonry of Chimay, enough to finance further travels. He returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1395 but seemed disappointed by changes he saw as the end of chivalry. The date and circumstances of his death are unknown.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Froissart wrote Chronicles, Meliador, a long Arthurian romance, and a large body of poetry, both short lyrical forms and longer narrative poems, among them L'Horloge amoureux. For centuries the Chronicles have been recognised as the chief expression of the chivalric revival of the 14th-century kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, and they are an important source for the first half of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. They survive in more than 100 illuminated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>. One of the most lavish was commissioned by the Flemish nobleman Louis de Gruuthuse in the 1470s, and its four volumes hold 112 miniatures by Flemish artists. Froissart is thought to have been among the first to mention the verge escapement in European clockworks, by 1368.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jean Froissart” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Philippa of Hainaut, queen of Edward III, took the Valenciennes chronicler <b>Jean Froissart</b> into her service, giving him access to the English court for his chronicles.</blockquote><a href="/empire/county-of-hainaut">County of Hainaut · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valenciennes">Valenciennes</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Chimay, in what is now <a href="/countries/belgium">Belgium</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.36,3.52&amp;year=1381" aria-label="Valenciennes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Jean Froissart"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5192 5816h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5192 5816h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.36,3.52&amp;year=1381">What was here in 1381? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Englishmen on the one party and Scots on the other party are good men of war, for when they meet there is a hard fight without sparing, there is no ho between them as long as spears, swords, axes or daggers will endure…&#8221;</p><footer>Chronicles, the battle of Otterburn, 1388 &#183; tr. John Bourchier, Lord Berners, 1525 &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/basis/froissart-full.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…so that shortly each of them is so content with other that at their departing courteously they will say, 'God thank you'; but in fighting one with another there is no play nor sparing…&#8221;</p><footer>Chronicles, the battle of Otterburn, 1388 &#183; tr. John Bourchier, Lord Berners, 1525 &#183; <a href="https://sourcebooks.fordham.edu/basis/froissart-full.asp" rel="noopener">Internet History Sourcebooks, Fordham University</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
